To assess whether depth signal extends beyond handcrafted features, we fine-tune a pretrained language model end-to-end as a depth classifier using QLoRA \cite{qlora}.

\paragraph{Setup.}
We fine-tune Qwen3-0.6B-Base with LoRA rank 16, learning rate $2 \times 10^{-4}$, and 3 epochs, framing depth estimation as 5-class sequence classification (gen-0 through gen-4).\footnote{Evaluated on the original 6-generator subset; XGBoost 3-class F1\,=\,0.789. We expect 8-generator training to further improve given additional diversity; the comparison direction is preserved.}
For 3-class evaluation, we collapse predictions into gen-0\,/\,gen-1\,/\,gen-2+ post hoc.
All results use the same LOGO protocol as the main experiments.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{QLoRA end-to-end depth classification vs.\ XGBoost on handcrafted features, under LOGO evaluation.}
\label{tab:qlora}
\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{5-class F1} & \textbf{3-class F1} & \textbf{Per-class F1 (5-class)} \\
\midrule
XGBoost (7 features) & 0.501 $\pm$ 0.038 & 0.789 & --- \\
QLoRA (Qwen3-0.6B)   & 0.583 $\pm$ 0.010 & 0.905 $\pm$ 0.003 & 0.999\,/\,0.796\,/\,0.413\,/\,0.259\,/\,0.447 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Results.}
QLoRA achieves 3-class LOGO F1\,=\,0.905 ($\pm$0.003), a +11.6\,pp improvement over the XGBoost baseline (0.789).
On 5-class evaluation, QLoRA reaches F1\,=\,0.583 ($\pm$0.010) vs.\ 0.501 for XGBoost (+8.2\,pp), with the largest gains at gen-1 (0.796 vs.\ 0.634) and gen-2 (0.413 vs.\ 0.268).
The gen-2/gen-3 boundary remains the primary failure mode (gen-3 F1\,=\,0.259), consistent with the feature-level analysis in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\paragraph{Hyperparameter robustness.}
A second run with LoRA rank 32 and seed 123 yields 5-class F1\,=\,0.587 ($\pm$0.005) and 3-class F1\,=\,0.905 ($\pm$0.004), confirming stability across hyperparameter and seed variation ($\Delta < 1$\,pp on both metrics).

\paragraph{Implications.}
The substantial 3-class improvement demonstrates that raw token embeddings contain depth signal beyond what summary statistics capture; the seven handcrafted features are a strong but lossy compression of the full distributional information.
End-to-end fine-tuning exploits token-level patterns (e.g., positional regularities, n-gram repetitions) that aggregate features cannot represent.
This confirms that the depth signal documented in our main analysis is real and exploitable beyond engineered features, while the handcrafted approach retains practical advantages: interpretability, computational efficiency (no GPU inference), and the ability to reveal the differential depth response pattern that motivates the task formulation.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Why does shape remain approximately stationary?}
The softmax architecture and sampling hyperparameters that determine distributional shape are fixed across depths.
Recursive generation shifts context toward high-probability regions, reducing entropy mean, but does not alter the generation mechanism itself.
This account predicts approximate stationarity under standard decoding: sampling ablation confirms that shape measures remain stable when sampling noise is low ($t \in [0.5, 0.7]$, ${\leq}0.3$\,pp degradation), but stationarity weakens at $t = 1.0$ ($-6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}), where sampling randomness begins to dominate the systematic location shift.
The phenomenon thus reflects a tendency of standard decoding rather than an architectural guarantee.
Entropy std ($|\rho| = 0.17$) is a borderline case: its moderate correlation places it between the clearly drifting central-tendency features ($|\rho| > 0.60$) and the near-stationary tail decay ($|\rho| = 0.04$), consistent with a feature that partially captures location-dependent variance.

\paragraph{Prompt-type dependence.}
Continuation chains yield the strongest signal (F1\,=\,0.781 vs.\ 0.730 for elaboration), with cross-type transfer at 0.753 (Appendix~\ref{app:prompt-type}).
Semantic-preservation constraints attenuate drift, consistent with \citet{broken_telephone}: the more a task anchors the output distribution to the input, the less room recursive drift has to accumulate.

\paragraph{Practical applicability.}
At the document level, gen-1 per-class F1\,=\,0.63 (37\% misclassification) is insufficient for automated filtering but sufficient for corpus-level flagging, where error scales as $O(1/\sqrt{n})$ and aggregate depth composition estimates become reliable.
Error analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:error-analysis}) confirms that misclassifications concentrate in shallow gen-2+ samples where drift has not yet accumulated, consistent with DDR predictions.

\paragraph{The gen-1\,/\,gen-2+ boundary.}
The primary classification difficulty is distinguishing gen-1 from gen-2+: 86\% of binary errors are false negatives (gen-2+ predicted as gen-1), with depth-2 error rate at 48\% compared to 20\% for depth-4.
This asymmetry follows directly from DDR: the first recursive step produces the smallest location shift, so depth-2 text occupies a region of feature space that overlaps heavily with gen-1.
Larger generators exacerbate the overlap (Mistral-7B error rate 45\% vs.\ Pythia-1B 18\%), consistent with higher-capacity models producing text that more closely matches the statistical properties of human-sourced input.

\paragraph{Relation to model collapse.}
Model collapse \cite{curse_of_recursion,strong_model_collapse} describes degradation when models are \emph{retrained} on their own outputs.
Our inference-chain drift occurs without parameter updates, potentially representing an early, reversible stage of the same distributional narrowing.
Topic drift shows weak correlation with depth features ($|\rho| < 0.29$; embedding similarity between successive generations correlates with entropy drift at $\rho = -0.286$), confirming it mediates rather than confounds depth signal.

\paragraph{Granularity and robustness.}
Three classes represent the practical resolution ceiling: 5-class LOGO F1 drops to $\approx$0.50, with depths 2--4 mutually indistinguishable (Appendix~\ref{app:finer-depth}).
That said, QLoRA fine-tuning recovers 3-class F1\,=\,0.905 (Appendix~\ref{app:qlora}), confirming that richer signal exists beyond the seven summary statistics.
Under text perturbation, LLM-simulated sentence regeneration (50\% edit distance) causes comparable degradation to 5\% random word replacement (Appendix~\ref{app:human-edit}), confirming that depth signal resides in generative structure rather than surface token statistics.

\paragraph{Instruction tuning.}
Instruction-tuned models show substantially weaker fingerprints (OLMo-Instruct LOGO F1\,=\,0.338 vs.\ 0.763 base).
Length regulation and format constraints imposed by alignment compress the distributional variation that depth fingerprinting exploits, justifying our restriction to base models as the appropriate setting for isolating the phenomenon.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Depth Classification Performance}
\label{sec:results-classification}

The 7-feature ensemble achieves 3-class LOGO F1\,=\,0.762 (95\% CI [0.758, 0.766]; $N$\,=\,37{,}058) across eight generators from five families (Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}).
Because gen-0 seeds are shared across generators (\S\ref{sec:generation}), the 3-class metric may carry upward bias from memorized gen-0 patterns; we therefore also report a leak-free binary metric (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+): F1\,=\,0.694, CI [0.688, 0.700], which is unaffected by this sharing.
Per-generator binary F1 ranges from 0.633 (Mistral-7B) to 0.724 (Pythia-2.8B); the primary difficulty is distinguishing gen-1 from gen-2+ (Appendix~\ref{app:confusion}).
Larger generators show moderate degradation (Qwen3-8B: 0.692, Mistral-7B: 0.633) relative to the 0.694 average.

\paragraph{Cross-model generalization.}
Cross-model chains (randomly selected generator per step) yield binary F1\,=\,0.764 under 5-fold CV,\footnote{Cross-model chains use the original five-generator subset (Pythia, OLMo, Qwen); Llama and Mistral were added later for single-generator evaluation.} confirming that depth signal persists when text passes through heterogeneous generators.
Leave-one-family-out (LOFO) evaluation yields average binary F1\,=\,0.686 ($\Delta$\,=\,$-$0.8\,pp vs.\ LOGO), confirming transfer across architectural families under the strictest unseen-generator test.
Appendix~\ref{app:domain} further demonstrates that the signal generalizes across text domains (News, Academic, Code; binary F1 $>$ 0.73).

\paragraph{Length confound control.}
Length-matched subsampling yields binary F1\,=\,0.724 (vs.\ 0.770 unmatched; $\Delta$\,=\,$-$0.046); a random-subsample control at equal $N$ (F1\,=\,0.767) confirms the drop reflects deconfounding, not sample-size reduction.
Length accounts for $\approx$6\% of discriminative performance (Appendix~\ref{app:length}).


\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Family} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{3-cl F1} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
Pythia-2.8B     & Pythia  & 2.8B & 0.798 & \textbf{0.724} & [.707, .742] \\
Pythia-1B       & Pythia  & 1.0B & \textbf{0.806} & 0.718 & [.700, .735] \\
Qwen3-1.7B     & Qwen    & 1.7B & 0.772 & 0.709 & [.693, .726] \\
Llama-3.2-3B   & Llama   & 3.2B & 0.775 & 0.697 & [.680, .713] \\
OLMo-2-1B      & OLMo    & 1.5B & 0.781 & 0.695 & [.679, .711] \\
Qwen3-8B       & Qwen    & 8.2B & 0.714 & 0.692 & [.674, .708] \\
Qwen3-0.6B     & Qwen    & 0.6B & 0.780 & 0.691 & [.673, .709] \\
Mistral-7B     & Mistral & 7.0B & 0.682 & 0.633 & [.617, .648] \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textbf{Average (8-gen LOGO)}} & \textbf{0.762} & \textbf{0.694} & [.688, .700] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{8-generator LOGO macro-F1 for the 7-feature ensemble (bootstrap 95\% CIs, $n$\,=\,1000). Binary F1 (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+) is the primary metric; 3-class includes the shared gen-0 class (\S\ref{sec:generation}).}
\label{tab:logo-results}
\end{table}

Ordinal regression methods (CORAL \cite{coral_ordinal}, Frank-Hall \cite{frank_hall_ordinal}) achieve comparable F1 with overlapping CIs, confirming that 3-class granularity provides insufficient resolution for ranking-aware losses.

\subsection{Differential Depth Response}
\label{sec:results-differential}

Features exhibit \emph{differential depth response} (DDR): central-tendency and diversity measures drift monotonically with depth, while shape measures remain near-stationary, consistent with recursive generation inducing a location shift in the NLL distribution while preserving its functional form.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_differential_response.pdf}
\caption{Differential depth response: Spearman correlations between individual features and generation depth. Central-tendency and diversity features (hapax ratio, TTR, entropy mean) show substantial monotonic drift; entropy std ($|\rho|{=}0.17$) occupies an intermediate position; tail decay remains near-stationary ($|\rho|{=}0.04$).}
\label{fig:feature-drift}
\end{figure}

Pooled class-balanced Spearman correlations with depth confirm the split (Figure~\ref{fig:feature-drift}):
drifting features show substantial monotonic trends (hapax ratio $\rho = -0.63$, TTR $\rho = -0.61$, entropy mean $\rho = -0.60$),
while shape features remain near-stationary (tail decay $|\rho| = 0.04$); entropy std occupies an intermediate position ($|\rho| = 0.17$).

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
Under 10-fold stratified CV, the full 7-feature set (F1\,=\,0.773) significantly outperforms surface-only (F1\,=\,0.688) and reference-only (F1\,=\,0.713; McNemar $p \approx 0$; Table~\ref{tab:ablation}).
Reference-only features yield higher binary F1 (0.833 vs.\ 0.695 for the full ensemble) because surface lexical features introduce noise at the gen-1/gen-2+ boundary.
However, surface features are essential for 3-class classification, where they improve gen-0 discrimination by capturing vocabulary diversity patterns absent in reference-model entropy statistics (Full 3-class F1\,=\,0.773 vs.\ Reference-only 0.713).
Permutation importance: hapax ratio (0.35), entropy mean (0.23), TTR (0.18).
Leave-one-feature-out confirms 6 of 7 features contribute positively (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}).

\paragraph{Cross-family reference models.}
Alternative references (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B) achieve comparable LOGO F1 (spread 0.013; Table~\ref{tab:cross-ref}), with consistently negative entropy drift, confirming DDR is a property of recursive generation.

